# ONBOARDING

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The onboarding experience introduces the athlete to CAS.

Its objective is not only to collect data.

Its objective is to establish trust.

The athlete should understand:

- what CAS does;
- why CAS asks each question;
- how the collected information improves recommendations.

Every screen should answer one simple question.

---

# Core Principle

Ask only what matters.

Every question must improve future recommendations.

If a piece of information is not used by the decision engine, it should not be requested.

---

# First Impression

The first launch should feel calm.

Professional.

Competent.

Avoid marketing language.

Avoid exaggerated promises.

The athlete should feel they are configuring a professional training system.

---

# Welcome Screen

Display:

CAS logo

Short introduction

Primary button

Example:

Welcome to CAS.

Your personal combat performance system.

Together we will build training adapted to your objectives, your recovery and your progression.

Primary Action:

Get Started

---

# Estimated Time

Tell the athlete how long onboarding will take.

Example:

Approximately 5 minutes.

If optional sections exist, mention them.

---

# Progress

Display onboarding progress.

Example:

Step 2 of 8

or

25%

The athlete should always know how much remains.

---

# Data Collection Philosophy

Questions should follow a logical order.

Identity

↓

Objectives

↓

Training

↓

Recovery

↓

Equipment

↓

Confirmation

Each screen should ask only one topic.

Never present a long form.

---

# Athlete Identity

Collect:

First name

Age

Sex (when relevant for recommendations)

Height

Weight

Dominant side

Preferred language

Explain why each element matters when appropriate.

---

# Combat Discipline

Select one or more sports.

Examples:

Boxing

Kickboxing

Muay Thai

MMA

Brazilian Jiu-Jitsu

Judo

Wrestling

Krav Maga

Karate

Taekwondo

Self Defence

Other

Multiple disciplines should be supported.

---

# Experience

Examples:

Beginner

Intermediate

Advanced

Competitive

Elite

Avoid numerical levels.

---

# Primary Objective

Examples:

Increase strength

Improve explosiveness

Improve endurance

Build muscle

Lose fat

Prepare competition

General performance

Injury return

The objective should directly influence future programming.

---

# Training Availability

Collect:

Preferred training days

Available session duration

Sessions per week

Home / Gym / Mixed

Morning / Afternoon / Evening preference

CAS should adapt planning accordingly.

---

# Available Equipment

Examples:

Barbell

Rack

Bench

Dumbbells

Kettlebells

Resistance bands

Heavy bag

Pull-up bar

Medicine ball

No equipment

Recommendations should respect equipment availability.

---

# Injury History

Collect only relevant information.

Examples:

Current injury

Previous surgery

Chronic pain

Movement limitations

Explain why this information is requested.

The athlete should understand that safety takes priority.

---

# Recovery Profile

Collect:

Average sleep

Perceived stress

Daily activity

Occupation

Recovery limitations

Avoid excessive medical questioning.

---

# Optional Integrations

Offer:

Apple Health

Apple Watch

Garmin

Polar

WHOOP

Future integrations

These remain optional.

The athlete may continue without connecting anything.

---

# Explainability

Before generating the first program, explain how CAS works.

Example:

CAS does not generate random workouts.

Every recommendation is based on your profile, objectives, available equipment and future training history.

Future versions may also adapt recommendations according to recovery and performance.

This explanation builds confidence.

---

# Confirmation

Display a summary.

Allow edits before continuing.

The athlete should verify:

Profile

Objectives

Training

Equipment

Recovery

---

# First Recommendation

The first recommendation should feel personal.

Example:

Based on your objectives, CAS recommends beginning with a strength-focused introductory block.

Avoid generic messages.

---

# Empty Data

If the athlete skips optional information:

Explain the impact.

Example:

Adding your available equipment will improve exercise selection.

Avoid guilt.

---

# Layout

Each onboarding screen should contain:

Title

↓

Short explanation

↓

One topic

↓

Primary action

↓

Progress indicator

Avoid multiple unrelated questions.

---

# Typography

Screen title:

Heading 1

Explanation:

Body

Question:

Heading 2

Help text:

Caption

Primary button:

Body Medium

---

# Color Usage

Background:

Graphite Black

Primary action:

Performance Orange

Validation:

Recovery Green

Warnings:

Warning Amber

Errors:

Critical Red

The interface remains calm.

---

# Motion

Allowed:

Screen transitions

Progress updates

Selection feedback

Not allowed:

Decorative animations

Confetti

Long transitions

Respect Reduced Motion.

---

# Accessibility

Support:

Dynamic Type

VoiceOver

Large touch targets

Logical navigation

Every question should be answerable without relying solely on touch gestures.

---

# Privacy

Clearly explain:

What data is stored.

Why it is stored.

How it is used.

How it can be deleted.

Transparency is mandatory.

---

# Error Handling

If onboarding cannot continue:

Explain why.

Preserve entered information.

Never force the athlete to restart.

---

# Success Criteria

The athlete should finish onboarding:

Understanding how CAS works.

Trusting the recommendation process.

Feeling that the generated program is personal.

Not random.

---

# Do

Ask only relevant questions.

Explain why information matters.

Build trust.

Keep each screen focused.

---

# Do Not

Create long forms.

Ask unused questions.

Promise unrealistic results.

Use marketing clichés.

Overwhelm the athlete.

---

# Final Principle

Onboarding is the beginning of the coaching relationship.

The athlete should leave with confidence.

Not with fatigue.